From 39ad3fa73ecdd77d4d138c80c9cfa3c9bb868509 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Zheng <bluevisor@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:07:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] print: IPP Everywhere (PWG raster) driver, IPP transport
 over TLS

Most network printers sold today accept only PWG raster or Apple raster
over IPP ("IPP Everywhere" / AirPrint), not PCL or PostScript, so none of
Haiku's drivers could print to them.

New driver "IPP Everywhere" (drivers/pwg): renders each page with libprint's
GraphicsDriver and writes PWG 5102.4 raster: 8-bit sGray, sRGB or RGB,
the whole sheet with the printable area centered, line-repeat and
PackBits-style run-length compression. It asks the printer (IPP
Get-Printer-Attributes) for its resolutions, raster types and media the
first time and caches them as attributes on the printer folder
(pwg:resolutions, pwg:types, pwg:media, pwg:media-default), so Page Setup
offers only what the printer takes.

IPP transport: the hand-rolled HTTP/IPP 1.0 code is replaced by a small
IPP/1.1 client (transports/shared/IppClient) on BSocket/BSecureSocket.
It handles ipps:// URIs, upgrades to TLS when a printer answers "426
Upgrade Required" (Epson requires TLS), accepts the self-signed
certificates printers use, decodes chunked responses, and reports the
printer's status-message on failure. Print-Job now sends a
document-format matching the driver (image/pwg-raster for IPP
Everywhere, application/postscript for PS Compatible, otherwise
application/octet-stream; an "ipp:document-format" attribute on the
printer overrides it) instead of always application/octet-stream.
